                'title' => 'Lowest common ancestor of a BST',
                'problem' => [
                    'title' => 'Lowest common ancestor of a BST',
                    'description' => 'Insert the n values one by one, in the order given, into an empty binary search tree, then print the lowest common ancestor of the nodes with values p and q: the deepest node that has both of them in its subtree. A node counts as its own ancestor, so if p is above q in the tree the answer is p, and if p = q the answer is p.

To insert a value, start at the root. Go left if the value is smaller than the current node and right if it is larger, until there is no node in that direction, and put the new node there. Never rebalance the tree.

Input
Line 1: n, the number of values.
Line 2: the n values in insertion order, separated by single spaces.
Line 3: p and q, separated by a space.

Output
One line with the value of the lowest common ancestor.

Constraints
2 <= n <= 1000
-1000000 <= each value <= 1000000
All values are distinct.
p and q are both values in the tree. They can come in either order, and p can equal q.',
